#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114982 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "c0c08150-98f9-5677-a7a8-390cffc0c5b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4511" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114983 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8c1e5afa-0afa-56ee-bbfa-4e84c64093ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-4512" :question/problem "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/e49dcc022775cc8eb5317611a3fae4d21892a7c566989f67f65e5213ee5c6b9d.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114984 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "cb19dcff-bb9d-55dc-941b-48153ca62e7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4513" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053284/assets/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "921ae110-e4e7-510f-ab59-186a0cb3c973" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330494} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7c9b25-12fc-5ac1-805f-8e0c3b86794d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330495} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2491c8-3992-5033-a799-225d97c66391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and  III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330496} {:answer/id #uuid "6e261158-2749-58a8-a570-b61fcbae7353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330497} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae7a3d7-f990-5ac5-bbf2-932a489a4383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae7a3d7-f990-5ac5-bbf2-932a489a4383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330498} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0892a77-ce44-577c-a9a2-1c997811c395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a109e6d7-dc35-5583-9b59-06b02bed5042" :question/math-academy-id "q-127918" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-11 \\\\ y+7x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The coefficient matrix is not invertible\n- The determinant of the coefficient matrix is not zero\n- The lines $x+2y=-11$ and $y+7x=4$ intersect at infinitely many  points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c52999-8c6c-555e-8840-1cd4bab11dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330501} {:answer/id #uuid "9290c049-23b5-50a3-ade1-aaa4a9e2e85f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330502} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f4e796-77a6-5195-81f7-c8b6d298bd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330503} {:answer/id #uuid "ac575434-04fd-5792-ae1e-a074ceec0347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330504} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7d9395-c97d-578c-b0ac-aac62cc8e854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac575434-04fd-5792-ae1e-a074ceec0347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330504} :answer-field/id #uuid "6caa1713-bf48-55c7-9f1d-6bfe6fd74b09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6b29aaf-c2bd-56b4-b59d-21a4aad187e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-127943" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2c8e05a-6cd5-5551-9b7c-dd4f8a3cf373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330508} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f6329c-857b-51da-a283-3af4aed7ab18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330509} {:answer/id #uuid "80b597be-53d4-5c68-aa27-b24b8dc278bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330510} {:answer/id #uuid "22d7b11d-79d3-5939-b9c2-7aa091fceb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330511} {:answer/id #uuid "6224bc3b-a8b7-5133-9c0d-68d2a10a92ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22d7b11d-79d3-5939-b9c2-7aa091fceb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330511} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6698ce-979a-52dd-8459-f3a40fe2f7bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2d9404d-2d42-56c6-b91b-5351ccec57cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-128139" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$* $\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcfb8ec-67b9-52c2-a103-85ed57f59a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330515} {:answer/id #uuid "95d1279f-cb6e-59d1-bd60-616e11825c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has \n no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330516} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc6ae29-26fc-5d1d-836f-a161036544a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330517} {:answer/id #uuid "a4847cea-f8f4-5525-bf6d-3821634688e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is the solution to $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330518} {:answer/id #uuid "f096ebb6-a1ba-569e-9941-f0b783e9cf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bcfb8ec-67b9-52c2-a103-85ed57f59a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330515} :answer-field/id #uuid "deefcdbd-7194-5ea6-b70b-6b8a393ac02c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d9e985e-33c1-5d2f-8e17-571966f68cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-128154" :question/problem "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93e3195d-5707-5394-bfd7-b5bd392e9b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330522} {:answer/id #uuid "54ca543d-bb77-5ba7-b8ac-2e7eabcbbe01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330523} {:answer/id #uuid "efc2b2e5-7db6-51af-b3b6-1955fca2208f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330524} {:answer/id #uuid "b7250835-daaf-5fbf-9947-00fb66c3ccc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330525} {:answer/id #uuid "87c96bd7-cc94-5687-8641-a31f7fa6a9c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54ca543d-bb77-5ba7-b8ac-2e7eabcbbe01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330523} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d6749e-e8a9-5218-82f5-ccfb94ddd7ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43d1dafe-4373-5932-b9d3-13fb994a3f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-31907" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aa1170-72a8-5ddd-ada4-c72ba530b5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330528} {:answer/id #uuid "595d97c0-443d-517f-977b-22aa8ad4027c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330529} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6c4553-57fe-595c-8f16-3b5d63f30144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330530} {:answer/id #uuid "59a6079a-9467-5abf-a7a6-f9e3aaecd01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330531} {:answer/id #uuid "788edb01-cdb7-582d-b34c-6c95c2d19da9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is solution to $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae6c4553-57fe-595c-8f16-3b5d63f30144" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330530} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e45d52-b96f-565d-bba3-d921ae06510e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f256f6bd-b552-59e7-9a79-ba8eaef62652" :question/math-academy-id "q-31921" :question/problem "Consider the lines $2x+y=2$ and $x+ky=5$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $2x+y=2$ and $x+ky=5$ intersect at one point, then\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is invertible.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8b778a-3b92-5c07-8af0-7d29fc78a8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113373} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fe18df-a1ae-52a8-bfc7-1bdd9132a9eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113374} {:answer/id #uuid "1161c7d0-938d-5b24-8896-746e88d3b5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113375} {:answer/id #uuid "45ef9a3f-382b-5b41-b5a8-7253dbaac42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113376} {:answer/id #uuid "70b6db37-5082-501c-9a2a-2a1f85c62a5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45ef9a3f-382b-5b41-b5a8-7253dbaac42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113376} :answer-field/id #uuid "959a3ca2-a8e8-5aad-ae3a-192c68541afc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44e70f28-a4b9-56ce-989d-752d9ef0f612" :question/math-academy-id "q-32088" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66e2ad3-9381-528b-8d62-fef833a90543" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113704} {:answer/id #uuid "499006b0-f8ab-55ca-becd-4d4b299aacca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113705} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc83cc9-86c9-581c-971d-0e0a533005c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113706} {:answer/id #uuid "79320fee-2d9b-5b07-881c-392096f745eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113707} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd90075-27ee-52c3-a192-1879fc3137c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79320fee-2d9b-5b07-881c-392096f745eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113707} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8794eb2-5786-5bcf-a6ce-2ede11c4aa02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f74e266c-1efa-53d7-993c-e40799d4cc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-36073" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=b$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has an infinite number of solutions" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758251ab-346f-57df-a097-68fe0a41dfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330535} {:answer/id #uuid "75079328-34e6-56c2-8150-435b7aaaf8f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330536} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1194be-2f13-5c37-8b94-0e0c5ad4ab78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330537} {:answer/id #uuid "54363958-2e6b-57d3-a6ea-9bf26a2b9196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330538} {:answer/id #uuid "77f65922-a6d0-5885-92b0-d5a17190dbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77f65922-a6d0-5885-92b0-d5a17190dbf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330539} :answer-field/id #uuid "78abb2bd-f1f2-585c-91f4-f0cd52c63820" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b6e7f5f2-eeeb-5be2-b36c-1be10042948d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36075" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$\n- $⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76deb05-c6cf-5022-b51a-29cb7147b359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330542} {:answer/id #uuid "172bd930-0c4f-5aae-b73a-db9f072470f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330543} {:answer/id #uuid "e96f1161-f7fd-5d6c-ab71-41a05fa2aa96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330544} {:answer/id #uuid "dde877b7-8cd5-5ba2-a414-d96bbdffe51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330545} {:answer/id #uuid "1320ac4b-781c-515d-90b1-108ae5c49a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dde877b7-8cd5-5ba2-a414-d96bbdffe51e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330545} :answer-field/id #uuid "249c35f9-187f-5471-96f8-c49e31b9dd86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54e595a0-bd68-572c-a6b8-ed1a72564809" :question/math-academy-id "q-47657" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cb6f76d-7eda-51cd-93e3-2214576b89cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330549} {:answer/id #uuid "14b21b86-6f60-53ad-b6b7-0b9885c5201a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330550} {:answer/id #uuid "6b79828a-05f9-5f82-bf22-79959354a084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330551} {:answer/id #uuid "952962b3-ca0b-5ba7-aaa5-96353c9dbb08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330552} {:answer/id #uuid "dfa62b31-2447-5a15-a6df-1cd24ab7a9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b21b86-6f60-53ad-b6b7-0b9885c5201a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330550} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eba4313-70ef-5888-8cd1-b2e596838327" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29ae41f9-7bfa-5f0d-93f0-493d5760a182" :question/math-academy-id "q-73562" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- The system $Ax=b$ has no solution for some $2\\times 1$ column vectors $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f65f4c72-8687-5c8a-8dc9-c6b83cee52fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330556} {:answer/id #uuid "827629a1-86e6-53c3-b347-99a43602a9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330557} {:answer/id #uuid "d947b6e8-cc40-5180-80a5-b585f7cf4356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330558} {:answer/id #uuid "9943587a-94ff-51cc-8008-8b1dca873ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330559} {:answer/id #uuid "f335913c-e069-542c-9528-3821d5e845e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65f4c72-8687-5c8a-8dc9-c6b83cee52fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330556} :answer-field/id #uuid "797b0442-973d-5e1f-8d80-7a850cab05f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e56c62ca-7f2f-5a80-bbab-2723e2c7fe5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73563" :question/problem "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $Ax=0$ in the usual way. Given that the system has a unique zero solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The system $Ax=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements II  and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283cd890-ca0a-56c2-8503-e089690a9d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330563} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc81105-ed1e-5bd2-8715-12b26afd8439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330564} {:answer/id #uuid "45dc26a5-3586-538a-8d9f-4e6e6947b389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330565} {:answer/id #uuid "6903117f-7bfc-5291-82cf-a8f525f7d6e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330566} {:answer/id #uuid "230bf8f7-ce78-538b-9148-626c032770de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc81105-ed1e-5bd2-8715-12b26afd8439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330564} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4336250-6373-548c-93ca-b2653e9b0d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e36a6e4b-b2b2-5f73-af06-061f4729b3a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73565" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5377ee4b-8a29-54b8-b75e-795f2259686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330570} {:answer/id #uuid "954f2c77-f11b-56f9-beca-340af0d23e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330571} {:answer/id #uuid "6c98df9a-31f5-55e1-a09f-4f4cd8df24dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330572} {:answer/id #uuid "0e86979e-0170-5764-ba6d-bb6f6fb8ed76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330573} {:answer/id #uuid "36764740-bc96-512d-8b60-fa6648ebf46f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "954f2c77-f11b-56f9-beca-340af0d23e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330571} :answer-field/id #uuid "9be3d716-4ca8-5fb8-9fa7-226b3f320ec2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33d9b6a8-08d4-5b01-9855-80084a55a511" :question/math-academy-id "q-73570" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- The system $Ax=b$ has infinitely many solutions\n- The lines $x+2y=2$ and $4x+ky=0$ don't intersect" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =k-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k-8\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 8.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false.  According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a True Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eff50924-30cf-58ef-b6b1-cce9830abec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  has infinite solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330577} {:answer/id #uuid "2d43e36d-ba1c-5d11-9ba7-150ad53811f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330578} {:answer/id #uuid "15bca348-4fdb-5a60-b959-1e0627c63c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330579} {:answer/id #uuid "c2be130c-a55b-5b9e-afef-951879e6d2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330580} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9ea525-7e2d-5ab3-a445-fd804d4703b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$  has no solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d43e36d-ba1c-5d11-9ba7-150ad53811f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330578} :answer-field/id #uuid "eed586c5-6075-5923-ba00-ca02b5cddd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3aa6bf3f-a787-587a-a396-0aad162cd33d" :question/math-academy-id "q-73596" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x+2y=1 \\\\ -3x+ky=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true: \n\nIf the system's coefficient matrix is invertible then $\\text{__________}\\,.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements. \n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible then $det(A)\\ne 0.$    \n\nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the two lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2.$ \n\nFinally, notice that if $k=-1$ then \n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]=6\\cdot (-1)-2(-3)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, if $A$ is invertible,  then $k\\ne -1.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the system's coefficient matrix is invertible, then the lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$,  intersect at exactly one point.*"}]}
